# 16 bases, four exponents each drawn with the seed 202611172 from whole ones up to 100 and past it, fractional, imaginary and complex ones, then the exact powers and the cut, as on 2026-11-17. The answers are the true powers, asked of mpmath 1.4.1 at 600 bits and rounded once on 2026-11-18, the sign of a zero part the limit's from the side the base's zero part names (`(-8 - 0i)^-2` is `1/64 - 0i`); the edges, a nan base and a zero one, keep Annex G's values. Each part is held to within the seventh float's units in the last place of the answer's size: `4 |n|` for a whole exponent `n`, multiplied out, each squaring doubling the error so far; 4 for any other, through the C library's functions, which was 3 at most on this Mac and in the Linux arm64 container. Python's answers, which this test held until 2026-11-18, were 24 to 77 such units from the true power at 101, and glibc's and Apple's differed by 107.
